WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.870
Good morning John; it's Friday.

00:00:01.870 --> 00:00:04.920
This time last week, and this is a little
hard to believe considering the weather we're

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:09.320
having here in Missoula right now, I was pulling
out of Port Everglades on a beautiful, sunny

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:11.079
evening in a boat.

00:00:11.079 --> 00:00:13.419
And let's be honest, it wasn't really a boat.

00:00:13.419 --> 00:00:14.749
Technically, it was a ship.

00:00:14.749 --> 00:00:22.670
More technically, it was a 150,000-ton, 240-foot
tall, 1,111-foot long, 183-foot wide floating

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:27.500
luxury hotel with a climbing wall and a basketball
court and mini-golf and a Johnny Rocket's

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:31.280
and four swimming pools and precariously placed
hot tubs and several beautiful performance

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:35.970
venues and an arcade and a beautiful dining
room and a casino and weird, big, red dogs,

00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:39.200
and a mall, and a giant cake, and freaking
ice skating rinks!

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:43.860
A ship that burns 8,000 gallons of fuel per
hour, which it uses not just to power the

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:48.360
engines, but to distill sea water for us to
surf on, power over a dozen elevators, keep

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:51.079
the ice cold and the hot tubs hot.

00:00:51.079 --> 00:00:55.250
While environmentalist Hank is rebelling against
the very existence of this thing, scientist

00:00:55.250 --> 00:00:58.280
Hank wants to know, how does this work?!

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:00.820
How on earth was it ever even created in the
first place?

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:05.180
The massive technological and logistical inputs
required to keep it moving and floating and

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:06.180
safe and happy.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:09.780
The goal, in fact, seemed to be for us to
just completely forget that we were on a ship

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:13.080
at all, best to just imagine that you're in
a quite nice entertainment venue that just

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:18.200
happens to be experiencing the longest, most
laid-back earthquake of all time.

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:20.880
So I was on this ship for the fourth annual
JoCo Cruise Crazy.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:26.070
a floating nerd adventure featuring semi-celebrity
nerds like John Hodgman, Peter Sagal, John

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:29.880
Scalzi, Pomplamoose, Molly Lewis, the Doubleclicks,
Paul and Storm, Paul F. Tompkins, Grant Imahara,

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:35.460
Wil Wheaton, me, and, of course, Jonathan
Coulton, the JoCo of JoCo Cruise Crazy.

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:38.600
It was pretty clear from day one that the
biggest advantage of being on this cruise

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:43.369
was not all the luxury amenities and not the
free room service and not never being more

00:01:43.369 --> 00:01:48.280
than twelve feet away from a hot tub; it was
being locked on a boat with cool people, with

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:51.810
no escape except for watery oblivion!

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:56.290
Every day, John Hodgman sat in a hot tub for
two hours, answering questions; I got exposed

00:01:56.290 --> 00:02:00.119
to talents I never knew existed, got to meet
my idols, got to hang out with a bunch of

00:02:00.119 --> 00:02:01.119
awesome nerdfighters.

00:02:01.119 --> 00:02:03.710
And that's what all the people on the cruise
were there for.

00:02:03.710 --> 00:02:07.259
Yeah, we stopped in Caribbean islands and
swam with sting rays and snorkled and went

00:02:07.259 --> 00:02:11.590
on the longest zipline over water in the world
and saw this goat chewing cud on a grave in

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:17.560
Jamaica and, yes, those were cool experiences,
but it occurs to me that the real value of

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:24.180
this somewhat monstrous, technological marvel,
is to give us a kind of ancient, simplified

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:25.180
life.

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:29.840
A small community where you can walk everywhere,
seeing the same people every day, people that

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:33.129
share your values and interests and passions
and experiences.

00:02:33.129 --> 00:02:37.970
A place to be foolish and comfortable and
joyful and proud, and somehow, these days,

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:42.010
we require an awful lot of complexity to get
back to simplicity.

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:46.989
Now, I know that cities are valuable and that
the internet is lovely, and we can't live

00:02:46.989 --> 00:02:50.819
that sort of insular life everyday, but, I'll
tell you what, six days didn't even feel like

00:02:50.819 --> 00:02:52.099
long enough.

00:02:52.099 --> 00:02:55.120
Does it have to be on a boat that consumes
a gallon of gas every twelve feet?

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:58.240
Maybe not, but I don't know how else to pull
it off.

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:00.320
I will be thinking about that though.

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:04.420
I'll also be thinking about sunshine, because
this is a bunch of balls.

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:06.160
John, I'll- oh my god.

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:10.590
I can't even go there.

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:12.680
This is just a lake now.

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:15.430
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

